{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you for a keeping.</b> Meaning, it requires guarding according to the law of consecrated offerings, since the name of pesach was called upon it. Further, it alludes in the manner that they of blessed memory said (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 2:2) regarding the verse ״גּוֹי מִקֶּרֶב גּוֹי״ (Deuteronomy 4:34), that Israel in Egypt were somewhat entangled in the statutes of idolatry on account of their being among the Egyptians, and they further said (Shemot Rabbah 16:2) that Scripture alluded to this by saying ״מִשְׁכוּ״ — that they should draw away from idolatry. Now, their intention, they of blessed memory, was not to say, heaven forbid, that they were worshippers of idolatry, but rather that because they were among them, without their knowledge they would perform statutes of idolaters in particular details of garments and foods and customary matters, and therefore, besides commanding that they uproot from themselves those particulars that they resembled, El Elyon further devised wisely to uproot the aspect of evil from them and to make a repair for this sin, and He said that they should take the lamb, which is the idolatry of Egypt through which Israel had stumbling-blocks, and perform with it the commandment stated in the matter, and through this they would have a repair for the guarding against the sin of idolatry — and this is His saying <b>and it shall be for you for a keeping</b>, meaning, in place of the commandment of guarding against idolatry, when they conduct themselves with it according to the written law — and understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:57.840149448Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}